welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Marwan Mille washer a senior fellow  at the Jackson Institute for Global  Affairs at Yale and a Jordanian diplomat  and politician combated Irma washer  opens Jordan's first embassy in Israel  in 1995 from 1997 to 2002 he served in  Washington as ambassador negotiating the  first free-trade agreement between the  United States and an Arab nation he then  returned to Jordan to serve as foreign  minister and then Deputy Prime Minister  ambassador mawashi was at the Jordanian  Senate until March 2007 when he joined  the World Bank as senior vice president  for external affairs  most recently he wrote a book called the  Arab Center today we'll talk with  Ambassador muwah sure about the  palestinian-israeli peace talks and his  case for taking a regional approach  welcome ambassador muwah sure thank you  it's nice of you let's begin by having  you bring us up to date on the  palestinian-israeli peace talks what's  going on they have started after a long  you know hiatus for years they started  again last September but unfortunately  they're stalled once again over the  issue of settlements the Palestinians  want a settlement freeze before they are  ready to resume negotiations and Israel  so far has resisted giving such a freeze  okay do you think the direct talks  between the two parties will lead to a  permanent agreement I'm not hopeful that  direct talks just on the  palestinian-israeli track will lead to a  permanent agreement I think that one the  talks are still following an incremental  approach which has exhausted its  possibilities and no longer I think will  lead to a permanent agreement I don't  think the two sides have what the other  side  once nowadays because the  palestinian-israeli negotiating track  will not deal with some of the broader  issues like Hezbollah and Hamas and Iran  and finally I don't think we have much  time for an incremental process again  the number of settlements in the West  Bank is in you know is getting bigger  the number of settlers has increased  from two hundred and fifty thousand at  the time of the Oslo agreements  seventeen years ago to over five hundred  thousand today so time is not on the  side of a two-state solution  I'm afraid okay what are the key  problems to the approach the Obama  administration is taking I think the  Obama administration needs to enlarge in  the talks and bring in other regional  players both sides as I said before need  a regional safety net on their side the  Israelis want to make sure that in  return for what they will see as painful  compromises they are getting pleased not  with half the Palestinians but with the  whole Arab world and the whole region  and the Palestinians on their side in  return for what they will see as painful  compromises on their side also want to  know that they will not be called  traitors for compromising on such issues  as Jerusalem and refugees and therefore  also need a regional safety net such a  safety net can only be brought together  if the Arab states join the talks not  meaning that they will sit at the  negotiating table opposite Israel but if  the proper context is developed where  the Arab state operationalized their  Arab state initiative were all Arab  States committed to peace and security  with Israel once a Palestinian state is  established okay what do you think the  Obama administration should be doing I  think they should be talking to the  Saudis I think they should be talking to  the Syrians and I think that they should  be getting what I call endgame deposits  from all sides in other words if the if  each side is not able to put the red  lines on the table to the other side now  they can do this hypothetically with the  US president if in return they know what  they would get at the end such endgame  deposits I think and go a long way in  order to assure the other side of what  exactly they would be getting if they  you know abandon their maximalist  positions and offer flexible compromises  that can result in an agreement other  otherwise I'm afraid the negotiations  will go on forever just as they have  done over the last 17 years okay why  involve Syria and Saudi Arabia for  instance Syria because of one the Golan  Heights they have occupied territories  by Israel - because they support  Hezbollah today and without involving  Syria even if the Palestinians and  Israelis are able to strike out a deal  Syria will not stay idle if their own  interests are not taken into  consideration why the Saudis because the  Saudis are the only country in the Arab  world today which has the stature to  bring to the table all the Arabs in fact  most of the Muslim world to support such  an agreement they have done it in 2002  when they spearheaded the development of  the Arab Peace Initiative that I talked  about and they can do it now and assure  the Palestinians of a regional safety  net and the support of all Arab and  Muslim states any other countries do you  think should be involved Jordan for  instance well certainly all Arab states  but and Jordan has been very active in  both the development of the Arab Peace  Initiative and the diplomatic moves that  have taken place since the Arab Peace  Initiative but there is no question that  what holds the key in the Arab world  today is Saudi Arabia let's talk  specifically about the endgame deposits  can you give some specific ones for  example the Syrians in return for total  full withdrawal of Israel to the June 4  67 board  need to make sure that they and  logistical and financial and military  support to organizations like Hezbollah  the Saudis in return for an agreement  that includes East Jerusalem as the  capital of the Palestinian state this is  what the Saudis care most about they  should deposit that they would ensure  the support of all Arab and Muslim  states for the Palestinians and that  they would include Hamas and Hezbollah  in any eventual agreement under the  terms of the Arab Peace Initiative the  Arabs have committed themselves to  security guarantees for all states in  the region including Israel what that  implicitly means is that the  responsibility of turning Hamas and  Hezbollah into political organizations  becomes an Arab responsibility only the  Saudis can do this in the Arab world  today so you are arguing for a more  regional approach but is there any way  that that could also fail absolutely I  mean we have witness already the failure  of the unilateral approach for example  when Israel unilaterally withdrew from  Gaza we have witnessed failure of the  bilateral approach I think this is  because of a reason and the reason is  there are today needs by both sides that  can only be met if you involve the whole  region and not just the Palestinians and  Israelis can it fail of course it can  but I think that the chances of success  are much greater if we go to a regional  approach in fact I think the chances are  almost zero for a success of the  bilateral approach as we have known it  and why do you do you have any idea as  to why the Obama administration would  not be more flexible and open to a  regional approach it seems to your point  it's it seems ministration has  definitely warmed up to the idea much  more than the Bush administration did  certainly they believe in  the Arab Peace Initiative and the  importance of that initiative precisely  because it offers such a regional  approach they have asked the Arabs to  operationalize the initiative but so far  they are still being in my view way too  cautious about bringing in the region I  think they understand the logic but I  think they have not so far taken the  needed step to go that route because in  going to that route they would have to  get deposits from everybody including of  course from Israel and whether they are  ready to do that and basically prod all  parties including Israel to deliver  their endgame deposits is still an open  question do you think having studied to  this and lived it basically for so long  that there will be peace in our lifetime  and if so how would that look there must  be peace for two reasons basically one  is we who live in the region cannot  afford to be pessimistic if you give up  basically it's a self-fulfilling  prophecy so we cannot give up but the  other sort of more practical reason is  that all other alternatives are worse if  we don't have a two-state solution today  the next thing that you can expect the  Palestinians to do is ask for equal  rights within the state they live and  within Israel which is of course going  to lead to the demise of the basically  Jewish Israeli state as we know it today  today the number of Arabs and Israelis  in territories under Israel control that  is in Israel proper plus the West Bank  and Gaza is almost equal about 5.5  million for each side in ten years time  the number of Arabs will be greater than  the number of Israelis so if you only  look at demographics it would dictate  that unless there is a two-state  solution where the Palestinians get  their own state and Israel of course get  their own state unless we do that soon  then the alternative becomes a one state  solution  which presents all kinds of difficulties  for all involved  very good so you're you're thinking  pieces possible at some point second you  very good thank you so much for being  here with us today and sharing some of  your work  thank you for more information about  ambassador mawashi  and his work please visit our website at  yale.edu backslash macmillan report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you
 

